course: medicine
lesson: 45
title: Cricket and rugby, baseball and American football
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Four bat-and-ball and oval-ball games, four scoring systems. In cricket a hit over the rope in the air is 6, along the ground 4; in rugby union a try is 5; in baseball a run means getting round all four bases; in American football a touchdown is 6.
explain: Each game also has its own safety rule. A hard cricket ball means a helmet with a face guard; rugby passes go only sideways or backwards, and tackles never at the head; children start with tag rugby and flag football, pulling a tag instead of tackling.
explain: The most important rule is the same in every sport: after a knock to the head, if in doubt, sit them out. Seeing two balls, a headache, dizziness or just feeling odd can mean concussion, even with no knockout.
explain: In the UK there is no return to competitive sport for at least 21 days after a concussion, and only step by step: the brain heals more slowly than a bruise.
fact: In cricket the bowler tries to hit the stumps and the batter defends them, scoring runs by running between wickets 22 yards apart; an over is 6 balls and a team has 11 players.
fact: In cricket the bowler's arm mustn't straighten while bowling, so bowling is not throwing.
fact: A ball that crosses the boundary in the air scores 6; if it bounces or rolls over first, it scores 4.
fact: ECB guidance says young players must wear a helmet with a face guard when batting against a hard ball, and when keeping wicket up to the stumps.
fact: In rugby union a try, worth 5, needs the ball pressed down on or over the opponents' goal line; a conversion adds 2, and a penalty goal or a drop goal is 3.
fact: In rugby the ball may only be passed sideways or backwards; a forward pass gives the other team a scrum. Union has 15 players a side and league 13.
fact: Young children start with tag rugby, pulling a tag off a belt; in real tackling, contact with the head or neck is dangerous and banned.
fact: Headache, dizziness, double vision or feeling odd after a knock can be concussion, sometimes showing later, and most concussions happen without a knockout.
fact: UK guidance is "If in doubt, sit them out", with no return to competitive sport for at least 21 days and a step-by-step return.
fact: In baseball three strikes and the batter is out, four balls and the batter walks to first base; each side bats until 3 outs, over 9 innings, and a run scores when a player gets round all four bases.
fact: In American football a touchdown is 6; the kick after adds 1 or a two-point conversion adds 2; a field goal is 3 and a safety 2; the attack has 4 downs to gain 10 yards.
fact: Flag football, with flags pulled from a belt instead of tackles, means far fewer head knocks; it was reported it will be in the Olympics for the first time at Los Angeles 2028, five a side.
exercise: Harry and Aditya play five overs each. How many balls does each bowl? | 30 | 11 | 5 | 60
exercise: How many players in a full cricket team? | 11 | 6 | 15 | 9
exercise: How is cricket bowling different from throwing? | The arm mustn't straighten while bowling | The ball must bounce three times | You bowl with both hands | You must stand still to bowl
exercise: Thabo from Durban hits the rope, but the ball bounced first. How many runs? | 4 | 6 | 1 | 0
exercise: 2 sixes, 3 fours and 5 singles. How many runs? | 29 | 10 | 24 | 35
exercise: Ishaan wants to bat against a hard ball at the nets with no helmet. What does ECB guidance say? | Young players must wear a helmet with a face guard | Helmets are only for adults playing in real matches | Ducking is safer than a helmet | Only the bowler needs one
exercise: Rhys from Swansea carries the ball over the line but never presses it down. Try? | No, it must be pressed down to score | Yes, crossing the line holding it is enough | Yes, if he cwtched (hugged) it | Only if the crowd cheers
exercise: 2 tries, 1 conversion and 1 penalty in rugby union. Points? | 15 | 12 | 8 | 18
exercise: Ruby from Christchurch throws a long forward pass. What happens? | The other team gets a scrum | Ruby's team scores a try for a long pass | A penalty kick to Ruby for good passing | Nothing, forward passes are allowed
exercise: How many more players a side does rugby union have than rugby league? | 2 | 13 | 15 | 28
exercise: Darragh at tag rugby wants to grab a lad round the head. What's the rule? | Pull the tag; head contact is banned | High tackles are fine at tag rugby if you're gentle | Grab the head but not the neck | Tackle as hard as possible
exercise: Lucas: "I'm fine, I just see two balls." What should happen? | He stops playing, it may be concussion | He plays on, he wasn't knocked out | He rests five minutes and goes back on | He drinks water and carries on
exercise: After a concussion, how soon at the earliest can a child return to competitive sport in the UK? | 21 days, step by step | The very next match | 2 days | Never again, in any sport
exercise: Tyler from Texas misses for strike three. What happens? | He's out | He walks to first base | His team gets a run | He gets one more strike
exercise: How many outs does each team get across 9 innings of baseball? | 27 | 9 | 12 | 36
exercise: Brayden says a touchdown is 7. How many is it really? | 6 | 7 | 3 | 10
exercise: 2 touchdowns, 2 extra-point kicks and 1 field goal. Points? | 17 | 14 | 19 | 21
exercise: Kayla wants tackle football; her mom says flag till she's older. Why? | Far fewer head knocks | Flag is easier to score in | Flag has no rules | Tackle is banned in the US
test: Harry: "Proper Test match, this." It's 5 overs each. If a real over were 8 balls, how many would each bowl? | 40 | 30 | 13 | 85
test: Thabo: "Lekker shot, that's a six!" It bounced once before the rope. What would make it a real six? | Clearing the rope without bouncing | Bouncing exactly twice | Rolling slowly over the rope | Hitting the rope post
test: Ishaan: "It's grand, I'll just duck." What's the hard-ball rule for him? | Helmet with face guard, every time | Ducking is enough at the nets | Helmets only in matches | A cap is as good as a helmet
test: Rhys's team leads 10–8 and scores a try but misses the conversion. New score? | 15–8 | 17–8 | 12–8 | 13–8
test: Ruby: "Sweet as, try time!" She threw it forward, like in American football. Why is a forward pass wrong in rugby? | Rugby passes go only sideways or back | Rugby has no passing at all | Only kicks may go backwards in rugby | Forward passes are only for kickers
test: Darragh: "Ah, I'll grab him round the head, it's grand." What would happen in a real tackle at the head? | A risk of concussion, and it's a foul | A clean tackle and a free kick to his side | A penalty try for Darragh's team | Nothing, heads are tough
test: Lucas wasn't knocked out but feels odd and sees double. His mate says "you're grand, get back on." What's right? | If in doubt, sit them out | No knockout means no concussion, so he's fine | Go back on after a drink of water | Play on but stay out of all the tackles
test: A player had a concussion on 1 March. Earliest return to competitive sport, counting 21 days on? | 22 March | 2 March | 8 March | 1 April
test: Tyler: "Ain't no way, ump!" A batter gets four balls outside the zone. What happens? | He walks to first base | He's out | He gets a home run | He gets three more strikes
test: Brayden's team scores 2 touchdowns and both kicks are good. He says that's 14 "cos touchdowns are seven". Is the total right, and why? | Yes, 14, but it's 6 for the touchdown plus 1 for each kick | No, it's 12, kicks don't count | No, it's 16, kicks are 2 | Yes, 14, because touchdowns are 7 on their own
test: Kayla: "Flag till I'm older, that's so mid." What's a true fact for her? | Flag football is set to be in the Olympics in 2028 | Flag football isn't a real sport, just a game for little kids | Flag football has more tackles | Flag football is played 11 a side
test: 3 teammates are on base, then the hitter whacks it so far that all 3 and the hitter get round to home. How many runs? | 4 | 1 | 3 | 6
exam: It is given that a batter strikes the ball such that it crosses the boundary without first making contact with the ground. Determine the number of runs awarded. | Six | Four | One | Two
exam: Pursuant to the laws of rugby, identify the directions in which the ball may lawfully be passed. | Sideways or backwards | Forwards only, towards the try line | In any direction whatsoever | Backwards only, and solely by kicking
exam: Select the response consistent with the guidance applicable in the United Kingdom concerning a suspected concussion. | The player is withdrawn and returns to competition after no fewer than twenty-one days | The player continues unless rendered unconscious upon the field | The player returns after a rest of five minutes | The player returns at the subsequent match
exam: It is given that a team records two touchdowns, two successful extra-point kicks and one field goal. Ascertain the aggregate score. | Seventeen points | Fourteen points | Nineteen points | Twenty-one points
